# Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity Policy

**{{ org_name }}** -- a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }} (EIN: {{ ein }})

## 1. Purpose

{{ org_name }} ("the Organization") is committed to fostering an inclusive environment in which all individuals are treated with dignity and respect. This policy establishes the Organization's obligation to prohibit discrimination and harassment, provide equal opportunity, and ensure compliance with applicable federal, state, and local civil rights laws. Adherence to this policy is a condition of every federal award received by the Organization.

## 2. Non-Discrimination Statement

{{ org_name }} does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, disability, age, religion, veteran status, genetic information, marital status, or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. This commitment applies to all of the Organization's programs, research activities, employment practices, board governance, partnerships, and federally funded activities.

## 3. Scope and Applicability

This policy applies to:

- All directors, officers, employees, volunteers, contractors, and consultants of the Organization;
- All applicants for employment, board membership, or volunteer positions;
- All participants in, and beneficiaries of, the Organization's programs, research activities, and services;
- All partnerships, sub-awards, and collaborative arrangements entered into by the Organization; and
- All activities funded in whole or in part by federal awards from any agency, including but not limited to NSF, NIH, DOE, and DARPA.

As a recipient of federal financial assistance, the Organization is required to comply with Title VI of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Age Discrimination Act of 1975, and their implementing regulations, as well as Executive Orders 11246 and 13672, as applicable. These requirements flow down to all sub-recipients and sub-awardees.

## 4. Equal Opportunity in Employment

The Organization provides equal opportunity in all aspects of employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotion, compensation, benefits, training, discipline, and termination. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and organizational needs -- never on protected characteristics.

## 5. Reasonable Accommodations

The Organization provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities in accordance with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, unless doing so would impose an undue hardship on the Organization's operations. Individuals who need an accommodation should contact the Board Chair or Executive Director.

The Organization also provides reasonable accommodations for sincerely held religious beliefs, observances, and practices, unless doing so would impose an undue hardship. Requests for religious accommodations should be directed to the Board Chair or Executive Director.

## 6. Harassment Prohibition

The Organization prohibits harassment based on any protected characteristic listed in Section 2. Harassment includes unwelcome conduct -- whether verbal, physical, or visual -- that is sufficiently severe or pervasive to create a hostile, intimidating, or offensive environment, or that unreasonably interferes with an individual's work performance, research participation, or ability to benefit from the Organization's programs.

Sexual harassment, including unwelcome sexual advances, requests for sexual favors, and other verbal or physical conduct of a sexual nature, is strictly prohibited.

## 7. Complaint Procedures

Any individual who believes they have been subjected to discrimination, harassment, or retaliation in violation of this policy should report the matter promptly using one of the following channels:

1. **Board Chair**: Contact the Board Chair in writing.
2. **Executive Director**: Contact the Executive Director in writing.
3. **Any Board Member**: If the concern involves the Board Chair or Executive Director, the report may be directed to any current member of the Board of Directors.

Reports should describe the conduct at issue, the individuals involved, relevant dates, and any supporting information. Anonymous reports will be accepted and investigated to the extent practicable.

Upon receipt of a complaint, the Organization shall:

1. Acknowledge receipt within five (5) business days, unless the report was made anonymously;
2. Conduct a prompt and impartial investigation, which shall be completed within thirty (30) calendar days of receipt, or as soon as reasonably practicable if additional time is needed;
3. Take appropriate corrective action where a violation is substantiated; and
4. Communicate the outcome to the complainant to the extent appropriate and permitted by law.

The Organization may retain outside counsel or an independent investigator at any point in the process. Nothing in this policy prevents an individual from filing a complaint directly with the relevant federal agency (e.g., the Office for Civil Rights at the U.S. Department of Education, the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, or the funding agency's Office of Inspector General).

## 8. Retaliation Protection

The Organization strictly prohibits retaliation against any individual who, in good faith, reports a suspected violation of this policy, participates in an investigation, or opposes conduct they reasonably believe to be discriminatory. Retaliation includes any adverse action -- such as demotion, termination, harassment, intimidation, or threats -- taken because of a protected activity.

Any person who engages in retaliation will be subject to disciplinary action up to and including termination of employment or removal from the Board of Directors.

## 9. International Operations

{{ org_name }} applies this policy globally, including to personnel, collaborators, and program participants in Pakistan and other international locations where the Organization conducts research or programmatic activities. Where local law imposes requirements that differ from this policy, the Organization will comply with local law while upholding the principles of non-discrimination and equal opportunity to the greatest extent permitted.

## 10. Posting and Distribution

This policy shall be:

- Distributed to all directors, officers, employees, volunteers, and contractors upon the commencement of their relationship with the Organization;
- Included in onboarding materials for new personnel;
- Posted in a location accessible to all members of the Organization (physical or electronic); and
- Referenced in all federally funded sub-award agreements and partnership contracts, as required by applicable federal regulations.

## 11. Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy at least annually to ensure it remains current with applicable law, reflects the Organization's activities, and is being effectively implemented. This review may take place at any regular or special board meeting and shall be noted in the meeting minutes.

